[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m To find the least possible value of \( f(X) = AX + BX + CX + DX + EX \) for a convex pentagon \( ABCDE \) with given side lengths and angles, we need to consider the Fermat point (or Torricelli point) of the triangle \( BCE \). The Fermat point is the point that minimizes the sum of the distances to the vertices of the triangle when all angles are less than \( 120^\circ \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Given that \( \angle B = \angle E = 60^\circ \), we can use the properties of the Fermat point. The Fermat point of a triangle with all angles less than \( 120^\circ \) is such that the angles between the lines connecting the point to the vertices of the triangle are all \( 120^\circ \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m We can construct an equilateral triangle \( BEF \) outside the triangle \( BCE \) with \( \angle BEF = \angle CBE = \angle BFE = 60^\circ \). The point \( X \) that minimizes \( f(X) \) is the Fermat point of the triangle \( BCE \), which is the point where the lines \( AB \), \( EF \), and \( CD \) intersect.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m To find the value of \( f(X) \), we can use the fact that the sum of the distances from the Fermat point to the vertices of the triangle is equal to the sum of the lengths of the sides of the triangle plus the length of the third side of an equilateral triangle constructed on the triangle.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m The sum of the distances from the Fermat point to the vertices of the triangle \( BCE \) is \( BC + BE + CE = 7 + 24 + 7 = 38 \). The length of the side of the equilateral triangle constructed on the triangle \( BCE \) is the length of \( BE \), which is 24. Therefore, the sum of the distances from the Fermat point to the vertices of the triangle \( BCE \) is \( 38 + 24 = 62 \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
