[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m To find the least possible value of \( f(X) = AX + BX + CX + DX + EX \) for a convex pentagon \( ABCDE \) with given side lengths and angles, we can use the concept of the Fermat point. However, since the angles at \( B \) and \( E \) are \( 60^\circ \), we can use the fact that the Fermat point in a triangle with one \( 60^\circ \) angle is simply the vertex of the \( 60^\circ \) angle itself. This is because the Fermat point minimizes the sum of distances to the vertices of a triangle when all angles are less than \( 120^\circ \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m We can split the pentagon into two triangles and consider the points \( B \) and \( E \) as potential candidates for minimizing the function \( f(X) \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
