[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m However, instead of considering just the triangles separately, we can use the fact that the sum of distances from any point inside a convex pentagon to its vertices is minimized when the point is the Fermat point of the pentagon, which in this case can be simplified to considering the vertices \( B \) and \( E \) as potential candidates.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Let's calculate the distances from \( B \) to the other vertices of the pentagon:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m - \( AB = 14 \)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m - \( BC = 7 \)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m - \( CD = 24 \)
