[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m To find the least possible value of \( f(X) = AX + BX + CX + DX + EX \) for a point \( X \) in the plane, we need to recognize that the minimum value of \( f(X) \) is achieved when \( X \) is the Fermat point (or Torricelli point) of the pentagon. The Fermat point is the point that minimizes the sum of the distances to the vertices of the polygon. For a polygon with angles of 60 degrees, the Fermat point coincides with the point where the angles subtended by the sides at the point are all 120 degrees.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m However, in this specific problem, we can use the fact that the pentagon can be divided into triangles and the properties of the Fermat point. We will use the reflection method to find the minimum value of the sum of distances.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m First, we reflect the vertices \( B \) and \( E \) across the lines \( AB \) and \( AE \) respectively to get points \( B' \) and \( E' \). The reflections will help us to simplify the problem by transforming it into a straight-line distance problem.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 1. Reflect \( B \) across \( AB \) to get \( B' \). Since \( \angle B = 60^\circ \), the reflection will form an equilateral triangle \( ABB' \) with side length 14. So, \( B' \) will be such that \( AB' = 14 \) and \( \angle B'AB = 120^\circ \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 2. Reflect \( E \) across \( AE \) to get \( E' \). Since \( \angle E = 60^\circ \), the reflection will form an equilateral triangle \( AEE' \) with side length 26. So, \( E' \) will be such that \( AE' = 26 \) and \( \angle E'AE = 120^\circ \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Now, the problem reduces to finding the straight-line distance from \( A \) to \( C \) via \( B' \) and \( E' \) and then adding the distances \( AX \) for the other vertices.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m The minimum value of \( f(X) \) is the straight-line distance from \( A \) to \( C \) via the reflections, which is the same as the distance from \( A \) to \( C \) plus the distances from \( B \) and \( E \) to their respective reflections.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m The straight-line distance from \( A \) to \( C \) via \( B' \) and \( E' \) is the same as the sum of the distances from \( A \) to \( C \) through the reflections. This can be calculated using the Law of Cosines in the appropriate triangles.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m However, a simpler approach is to use the fact that the minimum value of the sum of distances from a point to the vertices of a polygon with angles of 60 degrees is the sum of the lengths of the sides of the polygon minus the side opposite the 60-degree angle.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m So, the minimum value of \( f(X) \) is the sum of the lengths of the sides of the pentagon minus the side opposite the 60-degree angle, which is \( AB + BC + CD + DE + EA - CD \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Thus, the minimum value of \( f(X) \) is \( 14 + 7 + 24 + 13 + 26 - 24 = 60 \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Therefore, the least possible value of \( f(X) \) is \( 60 + 0\sqrt{p} \), where \( m = 60 \), \( n = 0 \), and \( p = 3 \) (since the problem specifies that \( p \) is not divisible by the square of any prime).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m So, \( m + n + p = 60 + 0 + 3 = 63 \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m The answer is \(\boxed{63}\).
