[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of ways to choose 8 chairs out of 16 such that no two chosen chairs are adjacent. We can approach this by considering the gaps created by the chosen and unchosen chairs.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Let's denote the chosen chairs by \( C \) and the unchosen chairs by \( U \). If we choose 8 chairs out of 16 such that no two chosen chairs are adjacent, we can think of placing 8 \( C \)'s and 8 \( U \)'s in a line such that there is at least one \( U \) between any two \( C \)'s.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m To achieve this, we can first place the 8 \( C \)'s with a mandatory \( U \) between each pair of \( C \)'s. This arrangement will look like \( CUCUCUCUCUCUCUCU \). This uses up 8 \( C \)'s and 7 \( U \)'s, leaving us with 1 additional \( U \) that can be placed in any of the 9 gaps created by the \( C \)'s (before the first \( C \), between each pair of \( C \)'s, or after the last \( C \)).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m So, we need to choose 1 position out of these 9 gaps to place the additional \( U \). The number of ways to do this is given by the number of combinations of 9 items taken 1 at a time, which is \( \binom{9}{1} = 9 \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Therefore, the number of ways to choose 8 chairs out of 16 such that no two chosen chairs are adjacent is 9. The remainder when 9 is divided by 1000 is simply 9.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=439428)[0m Thus, the answer is \(\boxed{009}\).
